From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hero Siege Offline Toolkit <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] Validate the_functions candidates instead of trusting the
 first mov

Why: On Hero Siege (GameMaker YYC x64) a fresh build of v4.0.1 dies during
stage 2 init, before the game window is usable. YYC::GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64
returns the first 7-byte "mov r64, [mem]" in Code_Function_Find without ever
looking at what it loads, and YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize then dereferences
that value twice, unguarded. Inferred from the log (there is no dump): on this
runner the first such mov most likely loads some other global. The shipped
binary carried a candidate filter for exactly this, but its source is lost;
this is an original re-implementation, not a reconstruction of it. The global
is now positively identified before anything trusts it: it lies in the game
image and holds an aligned pointer, the first 8 entries parse under ONE layout
(24 byte referential or 80 byte embedded) with a terminated graphic ASCII name
and a routine that is executable game image code, and the game's own
Code_Function_Find agrees on at least one of those names and contradicts none.
All matching RIP-relative movs are candidates, each distinct one is looked at
once, the first valid one wins. VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray validates the same
way (lowest valid one wins), since stage 2 falls back to it the moment the YYC
variant fails, also on a YYC game; it gets the same RIP-relative filter and
ZydisCalcAbsoluteAddress status check, so a mov that is not RIP-relative no
longer shows up as a candidate at address 0. Both finders hand their
candidates to one function, GmpiSelectFunctionsArray, which is the code the
host test runs. YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize reads through guarded copies, so
both of its call sites are safe on their own, and each of its refusals now
says which check failed. The x86 variant is left as upstream.

Evidence: evidence/fresh-build-crash.YYToolkit.log ends at line 736 with
"[...Interface.cpp:454] YYC::GmpFindFunctionsArray() => AURIE_SUCCESS,
0x00007FF7E35B6B80" and never prints the "m_FunctionEntrySize" line that
follows at Interface.cpp:463; CmWriteLogOutput flushes every line
(Console.cpp:76-77), and the only code in between is
YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize (Interface.cpp:132-146). The shipped DLL has the
strings "GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64() rejected candidate 0x%p" / "accepted
candidate 0x%p" (shipped-strings.txt:2404-2405) and breadcrumbs bracketing
that same call (:2121-2122). Host test hs-tests/fnarray_validation_test.cpp:
118 checks pass. A vtable-bearing object and a struct that starts like one
entry are rejected although the unpatched layout rule takes both. The
candidate choice is tested through the shipped function, not a model of it:
order versus address, a rejected candidate below the valid one, a candidate
listed twice, nothing valid, an empty and a missing list. 16 single-line
mutations of GmpiSelectFunctionsArray and GmpiIsSearchWorthRepeating each
fail the test (an unmutated control passes). For the validator itself the
first round measured eleven of twelve mutations failing a check; the validator
is unchanged since, the twelfth is noted below. NOT verified: the fault site is
inferred, there is no crash dump or fault address. What the wrongly picked
global is, how many candidates this exe has, and whether the real mov lies
within the window (widened from 0x200 to at most 0x400 bytes, clamped to
readable memory, still capped by GmpDisassemble's 0xFF instructions without a
LEA) are unknown until the first launch. Verified by reading only, because it
needs Zydis: which instructions become candidates (the RIP-relative filter and
the ZydisCalcAbsoluteAddress status check in both finders), and that each
finder writes its OUT parameter only after the selection succeeded. ASSUMED:
the first 8 builtins of this runner all have a routine inside the game image;
a search that failed three times does not start to succeed later. Not in the
test's reach: removing the VirtualQuery pre-check in front of
ReadProcessMemory changes no observable result on Windows 11 26200
(ReadProcessMemory alone also failed on a guard page and left it armed); it
stays as belt and braces. GmpDisassemble's use of an uninitialised record
after a failed decode is upstream behaviour and is not changed here. The other
stage 2 finders use the same first-match heuristic and were never reached by
the fresh build, so their behaviour on this game is unknown.

Fails-safe: No candidate passes => one rejection line per distinct candidate
with its reason, one summary line, AURIE_OBJECT_NOT_FOUND, and the OUT
parameter untouched (m_FunctionsArray stays nullptr). The VM fallback then
rejects the same candidates again under its own prefix and adds its own
summary line, and init ends with "Failed to determine function array size!"
and the VM finder's status, not a fault. No read in the validator can fault:
every read is a VirtualQuery region walk followed by ReadProcessMemory on the
current process, names are validated in a local copy, and guard pages are
refused untouched. The validator takes a referential table whose names are
merely readable, while YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize (upstream rule, unchanged)
wants entry 0's name inside a section called .rdata or its routine inside
.text. If the two disagree, a finder succeeds and the size is still 0: the
"YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize() refused" line then names the pointers and the
section bounds (or the section lookup that failed), and the error no longer
carries a success status, it is AURIE_MODULE_INTERNAL_ERROR. Log volume is
bounded per stage 2 attempt by the scan windows (at most 0x400 / 7 candidates
for the YYC finder plus 0x200 / 7 for the VM finder, each reason capped at
1024 characters). ModuleMain.cpp enters stage 2 again for every module that
is initialized while it is incomplete, so a failed search is repeated: at
most 3 times in full, each repetition announced by a "Functions array search
attempt" line, after that one "Functions array search not repeated" line per
further entry. Whether Aurie still delivers those callbacks once YYTK's own
ModuleInitialize has failed is NOT verified. Nothing runs per frame, no lock
and no file is involved.

Log-markers: "GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64() rejected candidate"
Log-markers: "GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64() accepted candidate"
Log-markers: "GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64() => none of"
Log-markers: "GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64() refused to disassemble"
Log-markers: "VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray() rejected candidate"
Log-markers: "VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray() accepted candidate"
Log-markers: "VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray() refused to disassemble"
Log-markers: "VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray() => none of"
Log-markers: "VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray() => no candidate in"
Log-markers: "YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize() refused"
Log-markers: "names confirmed by Code_Function_Find"
Log-markers: "Functions array search not repeated"
Log-markers: "Functions array search attempt"

Upstream-status: not submitted. Upstream v4.0.1 has the unvalidated first-match pick in both finders and the unguarded dereferences; later upstream history was not available offline and was not checked.

diff --git a/YYToolkit/hs-tests/fnarray_validation_test.cpp b/YYToolkit/hs-tests/fnarray_validation_test.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2938f65
--- /dev/null
+++ b/YYToolkit/hs-tests/fnarray_validation_test.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,790 @@
+// Host test for source/YYTK/Module Internals/GameMaker/Generic/FunctionsArrayValidation.hpp
+//
+// No game, no framework, no YYTK headers. This executable's own image plays the role
+// of the game image: its functions are the "builtin routines", its string literals are
+// the "name literals", its globals are the "the_functions" candidates.
+//
+// Build and run (x64 developer prompt), from the YYToolkit directory:
+//   cl /nologo /std:c++latest /EHsc /W4 hs-tests\fnarray_validation_test.cpp && fnarray_validation_test.exe
+//
+// Prints one line per check, returns non-zero if any check failed.
+// Every predicate has a positive and a negative control, so that neither
+// "rejects everything" nor "accepts everything" can pass.
+
+#include "../source/YYTK/Module Internals/GameMaker/Generic/FunctionsArrayValidation.hpp"
+
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <cstdlib>
+
+using namespace YYTK::FunctionsArrayValidation;
+
+#ifdef _WIN64
+static_assert(sizeof(ReferentialEntryLayout) == 24);
+static_assert(sizeof(EmbeddedEntryLayout) == 80);
+#endif // _WIN64
+
+// ===== Bookkeeping =====
+
+static int g_CheckCount = 0;
+static int g_FailureCount = 0;
+
+static void Check(
+	IN bool Condition,
+	IN const char* Description
+)
+{
+	g_CheckCount++;
+	if (!Condition)
+		g_FailureCount++;
+
+	printf("%s  %s\n", Condition ? "PASS" : "FAIL", Description);
+}
+
+static bool Contains(
+	IN const std::string& Haystack,
+	IN const char* Needle
+)
+{
+	return Haystack.find(Needle) != std::string::npos;
+}
+
+// ===== The fake game =====
+
+constexpr size_t TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT = 12;
+
+// "Builtin routines": executable, inside this image
+static int RoutineA() { return 1; }
+static int RoutineB() { return 2; }
+static int RoutineC() { return 3; }
+static int RoutineD() { return 4; }
+
+static void* GetRoutine(
+	IN size_t Index
+)
+{
+	switch (Index % 4)
+	{
+	case 0: return reinterpret_cast<void*>(&RoutineA);
+	case 1: return reinterpret_cast<void*>(&RoutineB);
+	case 2: return reinterpret_cast<void*>(&RoutineC);
+	}
+
+	return reinterpret_cast<void*>(&RoutineD);
+}
+
+// "Name literals": readable, inside this image. Deliberately not real GameMaker names,
+// nothing in the validator may depend on which builtin sits where.
+static const char* g_DefaultNames[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT] =
+{
+	"fn_alpha", "fn_bravo", "fn_charlie", "fn_delta", "fn_echo", "fn_foxtrot",
+	"fn_golf", "fn_hotel", "fn_india", "fn_juliett", "fn_kilo", "fn_lima"
+};
+
+// What the fake Code_Function_Find answers with
+enum FinderMode
+{
+	FINDER_EXACT,			// The index of the first entry with that name, like a real lookup
+	FINDER_KNOWS_NOTHING,	// Always -1
+	FINDER_OFF_BY_ONE,		// The index after the right one, which holds another name
+	FINDER_SCRIPT_RANGE,	// 100000 + the right index, as if every name was a script
+	FINDER_ONLY_ENTRY_FIVE,	// -1 for everything but entry 5
+	FINDER_FAR_AWAY			// An index way past the end of the table
+};
+
+static const char* const* g_FinderNames = g_DefaultNames;
+static FinderMode g_FinderMode = FINDER_EXACT;
+static int g_FinderCallCount = 0;
+
+static bool FakeCodeFunctionFind(
+	IN const char* Name,
+	OUT int* Index
+)
+{
+	g_FinderCallCount++;
+	*Index = -1;
+
+	int found_index = -1;
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT; i++)
+	{
+		if (!strcmp(g_FinderNames[i], Name))
+		{
+			found_index = static_cast<int>(i);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (found_index < 0)
+		return false;
+
+	switch (g_FinderMode)
+	{
+	case FINDER_EXACT:
+		*Index = found_index;
+		break;
+	case FINDER_KNOWS_NOTHING:
+		break;
+	case FINDER_OFF_BY_ONE:
+		*Index = found_index + 1;
+		break;
+	case FINDER_SCRIPT_RANGE:
+		*Index = 100000 + found_index;
+		break;
+	case FINDER_ONLY_ENTRY_FIVE:
+		if (found_index == 5)
+			*Index = 5;
+		break;
+	case FINDER_FAR_AWAY:
+		*Index = 90000;
+		break;
+	}
+
+	// The real one does not return anything meaningful either
+	return true;
+}
+
+static void SetFinder(
+	IN FinderMode Mode,
+	IN const char* const* Names
+)
+{
+	g_FinderMode = Mode;
+	g_FinderNames = Names;
+	g_FinderCallCount = 0;
+}
+
+// Candidates: globals of this image, like the_functions is a global of the runner
+static void* g_Candidate = nullptr;
+static void* g_CandidateSlots[6] = {};
+alignas(8) static unsigned char g_MisalignedStorage[32] = {};
+
+static void BuildReferentialTable(
+	OUT ReferentialEntryLayout* Table,
+	IN const char* const* Names
+)
+{
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT; i++)
+	{
+		Table[i].m_Name = Names[i];
+		Table[i].m_Routine = GetRoutine(i);
+		Table[i].m_ArgumentCount = static_cast<int32_t>(i);
+		Table[i].m_UsageCount = 0;
+	}
+}
+
+static void BuildEmbeddedTable(
+	OUT EmbeddedEntryLayout* Table,
+	IN const char* const* Names
+)
+{
+	memset(Table, 0, sizeof(EmbeddedEntryLayout) * TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT);
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT; i++)
+	{
+		strncpy_s(Table[i].m_Name, Names[i], _TRUNCATE);
+		Table[i].m_Routine = GetRoutine(i);
+		Table[i].m_ArgumentCount = static_cast<int32_t>(i);
+	}
+}
+
+static bool Validate(
+	IN void* Candidate,
+	OUT size_t& EntrySize,
+	OUT std::string& Reason
+)
+{
+	return GmpiValidateFunctionsArray(
+		reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(Candidate),
+		GetModuleHandleW(nullptr),
+		FakeCodeFunctionFind,
+		EntrySize,
+		Reason
+	);
+}
+
+// One committed read-write page directly followed by reserved (unreadable) address space.
+// The reservation is big enough that entry 90000 of a table on that page still lands in it.
+constexpr size_t EDGE_PAGE_RESERVATION = 8 * 1024 * 1024;
+
+struct EdgePage
+{
+	unsigned char* Base;
+	unsigned char* End;
+	size_t Size;
+};
+
+static EdgePage AllocateEdgePage()
+{
+	SYSTEM_INFO system_info = {};
+	GetSystemInfo(&system_info);
+
+	EdgePage page = {};
+	page.Size = system_info.dwPageSize;
+	page.Base = static_cast<unsigned char*>(VirtualAlloc(nullptr, EDGE_PAGE_RESERVATION, MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_NOACCESS));
+	if (!page.Base || !VirtualAlloc(page.Base, page.Size, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READWRITE))
+	{
+		printf("FAIL  could not allocate an edge page, giving up\n");
+		exit(2);
+	}
+
+	page.End = page.Base + page.Size;
+	return page;
+}
+
+// Something an unvalidated "first 7-byte mov" can very well load instead of the_functions
+struct PolymorphicDecoy
+{
+	virtual ~PolymorphicDecoy() {}
+	virtual int Touch() { return m_Value; }
+
+	int m_Value = 7;
+	void* m_Padding[64] = {};
+};
+
+// First qword points at a readable literal in the image, second one at code in the image:
+// exactly one plausible referential entry, and then nothing.
+struct SingleEntryDecoy
+{
+	const char* m_Text;
+	void* m_Callback;
+	uint64_t m_Counters[64];
+};
+
+// The rule this validator replaces, as a baseline: YkDetermineFunctionEntrySize settles for
+// "the first pointer-sized value of entry 0 points into the image". Test-only, and only ever
+// pointed at memory this test owns.
+static bool WeakRuleAccepts(
+	IN void* Candidate
+)
+{
+	void* first_entry = *static_cast<void**>(Candidate);
+	if (!first_entry)
+		return false;
+
+	return GmpiIsReadableImageAddress(*static_cast<uintptr_t*>(first_entry), GetModuleHandleW(nullptr));
+}
+
+// ===== Tests =====
+
+static void TestNamePredicate()
+{
+	char exactly_full[64];
+	memset(exactly_full, 'a', sizeof(exactly_full));
+
+	char longest_valid[64];
+	memset(longest_valid, 'a', sizeof(longest_valid));
+	longest_valid[63] = '\0';
+
+	Check(GmpiIsFunctionName("abs", 4), "name: 'abs' is a function name");
+	Check(GmpiIsFunctionName("@@NewGMLArray@@", 16), "name: punctuation is graphic ASCII too");
+	Check(GmpiIsFunctionName(longest_valid, sizeof(longest_valid)), "name: 63 characters plus terminator fit an embedded name");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName("", 1), "name: empty is rejected");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName(exactly_full, sizeof(exactly_full)), "name: 64 characters without a terminator are rejected");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName("abcd", 4), "name: terminator outside of the given size is rejected");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName("ab cd", 6), "name: space is rejected");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName("ab\x01" "cd", 6), "name: control character is rejected");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName("ab\x7F" "cd", 6), "name: DEL is rejected");
+	Check(!GmpiIsFunctionName("ab\xE9" "cd", 6), "name: byte above 0x7F is rejected");
+}
+
+static void TestReasonCap()
+{
+	std::string short_reason = "short";
+	GmpiCapReasonLength(short_reason, 16);
+	Check(short_reason == "short", "reason cap: a short reason is left alone");
+
+	std::string long_reason(5000, 'x');
+	GmpiCapReasonLength(long_reason, MAXIMUM_REASON_LENGTH);
+	Check(long_reason.size() == MAXIMUM_REASON_LENGTH, "reason cap: a long reason is cut to the maximum");
+	Check(long_reason.compare(long_reason.size() - 3, 3, "...") == 0, "reason cap: a cut reason says so");
+	Check(MAXIMUM_REASON_LENGTH + 512 < 4096, "reason cap: leaves room in CmpParseVa's 4096 byte buffer");
+}
+
+static void TestGuardedReads()
+{
+	EdgePage page = AllocateEdgePage();
+	memset(page.Base, 0x5A, page.Size);
+
+	// Spans
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 64) == 64, "span: fully inside a committed page");
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.End - 16), 64) == 16, "span: stops at the reserved page, counted from the address and not from the region base");
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.End), 64) == 0, "span: reserved memory is not readable");
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(0, 64) == 0, "span: null is not readable");
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(UINTPTR_MAX - 4, 64) == 0, "span: end of the address space is not readable and does not wrap");
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 0) == 0, "span: nothing wanted, nothing reported");
+
+	// A range that straddles two regions with different protections is one span
+	EdgePage split = AllocateEdgePage();
+	VirtualAlloc(split.End, split.Size, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READONLY);
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(split.End - 8), 16) == 16, "span: walks across two readable regions");
+
+	// Copies
+	unsigned char buffer[64];
+	memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(buffer));
+	Check(GmpiCopyMemory(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.End - 16), 16, buffer) && buffer[0] == 0x5A && buffer[15] == 0x5A && buffer[16] == 0,
+		"copy: the last 16 bytes of a page are copied");
+
+	memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(buffer));
+	Check(!GmpiCopyMemory(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.End - 16), 17, buffer) && buffer[0] == 0,
+		"copy: one byte into the reserved page fails, without a fault and without a partial copy");
+	Check(!GmpiCopyMemory(0, 8, buffer), "copy: null fails");
+	Check(!GmpiCopyMemory(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 0, buffer), "copy: size 0 fails");
+	Check(!GmpiCopyMemory(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 8, nullptr), "copy: no buffer fails");
+
+	// Protections
+	DWORD old_protection = 0;
+	MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION region;
+
+	Check(GmpiQueryRegion(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), region) && GmpiIsRegionReadable(region) && !GmpiIsRegionExecutable(region),
+		"region: PAGE_READWRITE is readable and not executable");
+	Check(!GmpiQueryRegion(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.End), region), "region: reserved memory is refused");
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_NOACCESS, &old_protection);
+	Check(!GmpiQueryRegion(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), region), "region: PAGE_NOACCESS is refused");
+	Check(!GmpiCopyMemory(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 8, buffer), "copy: PAGE_NOACCESS fails without a fault");
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_READWRITE | PAGE_GUARD, &old_protection);
+	Check(!GmpiQueryRegion(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), region), "region: a guard page is refused");
+	Check(!GmpiCopyMemory(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 8, buffer), "copy: a guard page fails without a fault");
+	VirtualQuery(page.Base, &region, sizeof(region));
+	Check((region.Protect & PAGE_GUARD) != 0, "copy: the guard page is still armed afterwards");
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_EXECUTE, &old_protection);
+	Check(GmpiQueryRegion(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), region) && !GmpiIsRegionReadable(region) && GmpiIsRegionExecutable(region),
+		"region: PAGE_EXECUTE is executable and NOT readable");
+	Check(GmpiGetReadableSpan(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), 8) == 0, "span: PAGE_EXECUTE is not readable");
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_EXECUTE_READ, &old_protection);
+	Check(GmpiQueryRegion(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), region) && GmpiIsRegionReadable(region) && GmpiIsRegionExecutable(region),
+		"region: PAGE_EXECUTE_READ is both");
+
+	VirtualFree(page.Base, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
+	VirtualFree(split.Base, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
+}
+
+static void TestImagePredicates()
+{
+	const LPCVOID this_image = GetModuleHandleW(nullptr);
+	const LPCVOID other_image = GetModuleHandleW(L"kernel32.dll");
+	const uintptr_t own_routine = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(GetRoutine(0));
+	const uintptr_t foreign_routine = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(GetProcAddress(GetModuleHandleW(L"kernel32.dll"), "VirtualQuery"));
+	int stack_variable = 0;
+
+	EdgePage page = AllocateEdgePage();
+	DWORD old_protection = 0;
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_EXECUTE_READ, &old_protection);
+
+	Check(other_image && foreign_routine, "image: kernel32 is there to serve as the foreign image");
+
+	Check(GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(own_routine, this_image), "image: own function is executable image code");
+	Check(!GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(own_routine, other_image), "image: own function is not part of another image");
+	Check(!GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(foreign_routine, this_image), "image: a kernel32 function is not part of this image");
+	Check(GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(foreign_routine, nullptr) == false, "image: no image base matches nothing");
+	Check(!GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_Candidate), this_image), "image: own data is not executable");
+	Check(!GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), this_image), "image: executable private memory is not image code");
+	Check(!GmpiIsExecutableImageAddress(0, this_image), "image: null is not executable image code");
+
+	Check(GmpiIsReadableImageAddress(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(g_DefaultNames[0]), this_image), "image: own string literal is readable image memory");
+	Check(GmpiIsReadableImageAddress(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_Candidate), this_image), "image: own global is readable image memory");
+	Check(!GmpiIsReadableImageAddress(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&stack_variable), this_image), "image: the stack is not image memory");
+	Check(!GmpiIsReadableImageAddress(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(page.Base), this_image), "image: private memory is not image memory");
+
+	VirtualFree(page.Base, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
+}
+
+static void TestAcceptedTables()
+{
+	size_t entry_size = 0;
+	std::string reason;
+
+	// (b) referential layout on the heap
+	ReferentialEntryLayout* referential_table = new ReferentialEntryLayout[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	BuildReferentialTable(referential_table, g_DefaultNames);
+	g_Candidate = referential_table;
+
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 24, "accept: referential table is accepted with entry size 24");
+	Check(Contains(reason, "entry 0 is 'fn_alpha'") && Contains(reason, "8 of 8"), "accept: the summary names entry 0 and the confirmed count");
+	Check(g_FinderCallCount == 8, "accept: the game was asked about each sampled name once");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+
+	// Only one name known to the game is enough, as long as nothing contradicts
+	SetFinder(FINDER_ONLY_ENTRY_FIVE, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "1 of 8"), "accept: one confirmed name and no contradiction is enough");
+
+	// A name registered twice: the game reports the first one for both
+	char duplicate_name[] = "fn_bravo";
+	const char* names_with_duplicate[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	memcpy(names_with_duplicate, g_DefaultNames, sizeof(names_with_duplicate));
+	names_with_duplicate[3] = duplicate_name;
+	BuildReferentialTable(referential_table, names_with_duplicate);
+
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, names_with_duplicate);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 24, "accept: a duplicate name resolving to the other entry is not a contradiction");
+
+	// (c) embedded layout
+	EmbeddedEntryLayout* embedded_table = new EmbeddedEntryLayout[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	BuildEmbeddedTable(embedded_table, g_DefaultNames);
+	g_Candidate = embedded_table;
+
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 80, "accept: embedded table is accepted with entry size 80");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+
+	// (i) a name literal whose terminator is the very last readable byte
+	EdgePage page = AllocateEdgePage();
+	const char edge_name[] = "fn_edge";
+	char* edge_name_address = reinterpret_cast<char*>(page.End - sizeof(edge_name));
+	memcpy(edge_name_address, edge_name, sizeof(edge_name));
+
+	const char* names_with_edge[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	memcpy(names_with_edge, g_DefaultNames, sizeof(names_with_edge));
+	names_with_edge[3] = edge_name_address;
+	BuildReferentialTable(referential_table, names_with_edge);
+	g_Candidate = referential_table;
+
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, names_with_edge);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 24, "accept: a name ending on the last byte before an unreadable page");
+
+	// (j) the same name without its terminator: nothing readable is left to find one in
+	memset(edge_name_address, 'x', sizeof(edge_name));
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 0, "reject: an unterminated name running into an unreadable page, without a fault");
+	Check(Contains(reason, "entry 3 has a name that is not a terminated graphic ASCII string"), "reject: ...and the reason names the entry");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+
+	VirtualFree(page.Base, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
+	delete[] embedded_table;
+	delete[] referential_table;
+}
+
+static void TestRejectedCandidates()
+{
+	size_t entry_size = 0;
+	std::string reason;
+
+	ReferentialEntryLayout* referential_table = new ReferentialEntryLayout[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	BuildReferentialTable(referential_table, g_DefaultNames);
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+
+	// Positive control for this whole block: the table everything below is derived from is fine
+	g_Candidate = referential_table;
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "control: the table the rejections are derived from is accepted");
+
+	// (e) null
+	g_Candidate = nullptr;
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "does not hold an aligned pointer"), "reject: a null global");
+
+	// (a) scalars, the kind of global a stack cookie or a counter is
+	g_Candidate = reinterpret_cast<void*>(static_cast<uintptr_t>(0x1122334455667788ull));
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "reject: an aligned scalar pointing nowhere, without a fault");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+
+	g_Candidate = reinterpret_cast<void*>(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(referential_table) + 1);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "does not hold an aligned pointer"), "reject: an odd value, even though it points into a valid table");
+
+	// The candidate itself
+	Check(!Validate(nullptr, entry_size, reason), "reject: a null candidate");
+	Check(!Validate(reinterpret_cast<void*>(UINTPTR_MAX - 7), entry_size, reason), "reject: a candidate at the end of the address space");
+
+	void** heap_global = new void*(referential_table);
+	Check(!Validate(heap_global, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "not readable game image memory"), "reject: a valid table behind a global that is not in the image");
+	delete heap_global;
+
+	void* table_pointer = referential_table;
+	memcpy(g_MisalignedStorage + 1, &table_pointer, sizeof(table_pointer));
+	Check(!Validate(g_MisalignedStorage + 1, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "not pointer-aligned"), "reject: a valid table behind a misaligned global");
+
+	memcpy(g_MisalignedStorage + 8, &table_pointer, sizeof(table_pointer));
+	Check(Validate(g_MisalignedStorage + 8, entry_size, reason), "control: the same storage is accepted at an aligned offset");
+
+	// Nothing to check against
+	g_Candidate = referential_table;
+	Check(!GmpiValidateFunctionsArray(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_Candidate), GetModuleHandleW(nullptr), nullptr, entry_size, reason),
+		"reject: no Code_Function_Find to ask");
+	Check(!GmpiValidateFunctionsArray(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_Candidate), nullptr, FakeCodeFunctionFind, entry_size, reason),
+		"reject: no image to compare against");
+	Check(!GmpiValidateFunctionsArray(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_Candidate), GetModuleHandleW(L"kernel32.dll"), FakeCodeFunctionFind, entry_size, reason),
+		"reject: a valid table of another image");
+
+	// (d) an object with a vtable. The baseline rule takes it, which is what makes this a control worth having.
+	PolymorphicDecoy* polymorphic_decoy = new PolymorphicDecoy();
+	g_Candidate = polymorphic_decoy;
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(WeakRuleAccepts(&g_Candidate), "baseline: the unpatched layout rule takes a vtable-bearing object for a referential table");
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 0, "reject: a vtable-bearing object");
+	Check(g_FinderCallCount == 0, "reject: ...and the game was never asked about its 'names'");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+	Check(polymorphic_decoy->Touch() == 7, "reject: ...and the object is untouched");
+
+	// One plausible entry followed by garbage
+	SingleEntryDecoy* single_entry_decoy = new SingleEntryDecoy();
+	memset(single_entry_decoy, 0, sizeof(*single_entry_decoy));
+	single_entry_decoy->m_Text = g_DefaultNames[0];
+	single_entry_decoy->m_Callback = GetRoutine(0);
+	g_Candidate = single_entry_decoy;
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(WeakRuleAccepts(&g_Candidate), "baseline: the unpatched layout rule takes a struct that starts like one entry");
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "entry 1"), "reject: a struct that starts like one entry, at entry 1");
+	Check(g_FinderCallCount == 0, "reject: ...and the game was never asked about it");
+
+	// (h) routines that are not code of the image
+	g_Candidate = referential_table;
+	referential_table[6].m_Routine = referential_table;
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "entry 6 ('fn_golf') has a routine that is not executable game image code"),
+		"reject: a routine on the heap");
+
+	referential_table[6].m_Routine = &g_Candidate;
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "entry 6"), "reject: a routine pointing at data of the image");
+
+	referential_table[6].m_Routine = reinterpret_cast<void*>(GetProcAddress(GetModuleHandleW(L"kernel32.dll"), "VirtualQuery"));
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "entry 6"), "reject: a routine in another image");
+
+	referential_table[6].m_Routine = nullptr;
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "entry 6"), "reject: a null routine");
+
+	referential_table[6].m_Routine = GetRoutine(6);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "control: accepted again once the routine is restored");
+
+	// (f) (g) what the game says
+	SetFinder(FINDER_OFF_BY_ONE, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "Code_Function_Find places entry 0 ('fn_alpha') at index 1, which holds another name"),
+		"reject: the game contradicts the table");
+
+	SetFinder(FINDER_KNOWS_NOTHING, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "confirmed none"), "reject: the game knows none of the names");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+
+	SetFinder(FINDER_SCRIPT_RANGE, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "confirmed none"), "reject: the game takes every name for a script");
+
+	// An embedded name that fills all 64 bytes
+	EmbeddedEntryLayout* embedded_table = new EmbeddedEntryLayout[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	BuildEmbeddedTable(embedded_table, g_DefaultNames);
+	g_Candidate = embedded_table;
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && entry_size == 80, "control: the embedded table is accepted");
+	memset(embedded_table[2].m_Name, 'z', sizeof(embedded_table[2].m_Name));
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "as embedded: entry 2"), "reject: an embedded name without a terminator");
+
+	// A table that ends early, right in front of an unreadable page
+	EdgePage page = AllocateEdgePage();
+	ReferentialEntryLayout* short_table = reinterpret_cast<ReferentialEntryLayout*>(page.End - (sizeof(ReferentialEntryLayout) * 5));
+	memcpy(short_table, referential_table, sizeof(ReferentialEntryLayout) * 5);
+	g_Candidate = short_table;
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "as referential: entry 5 is not readable"), "reject: a table of 5 entries in front of an unreadable page, without a fault");
+	printf("      -> %s\n", reason.c_str());
+
+	// The game points way past a table that is otherwise fine
+	ReferentialEntryLayout* edge_table = reinterpret_cast<ReferentialEntryLayout*>(page.End - (sizeof(ReferentialEntryLayout) * TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT));
+	memcpy(edge_table, referential_table, sizeof(ReferentialEntryLayout) * TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT);
+	g_Candidate = edge_table;
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "control: the table in front of the unreadable page is accepted");
+	SetFinder(FINDER_FAR_AWAY, g_DefaultNames);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason) && Contains(reason, "at index 90000, which is not readable"), "reject: the game reports an index past the mapped table, without a fault");
+
+	// (k) protections on the table itself
+	DWORD old_protection = 0;
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_NOACCESS, &old_protection);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "reject: the table is PAGE_NOACCESS, without a fault");
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_READWRITE | PAGE_GUARD, &old_protection);
+	Check(!Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "reject: the table is a guard page, without a fault");
+
+	MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION region = {};
+	VirtualQuery(page.Base, &region, sizeof(region));
+	Check((region.Protect & PAGE_GUARD) != 0, "reject: ...and the guard is still armed");
+
+	VirtualProtect(page.Base, page.Size, PAGE_READWRITE, &old_protection);
+	Check(Validate(&g_Candidate, entry_size, reason), "control: the very same table is accepted once it is readable again");
+
+	VirtualFree(page.Base, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
+	delete[] embedded_table;
+	delete single_entry_decoy;
+	delete polymorphic_decoy;
+	delete[] referential_table;
+}
+
+// What the reporter of GmpiSelectFunctionsArray was told
+struct SelectionLog
+{
+	size_t ReportCount;
+	size_t AcceptedCount;
+	size_t RejectedCount;
+	size_t EmptyTextCount;
+	uintptr_t Reported[16];
+};
+
+static size_t ReportCountOf(
+	IN const SelectionLog& Log,
+	IN uintptr_t Candidate
+)
+{
+	size_t hits = 0;
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < Log.ReportCount && i < 16; i++)
+	{
+		if (Log.Reported[i] == Candidate)
+			hits++;
+	}
+
+	return hits;
+}
+
+// Runs the very function both finders hand their candidates to
+static bool Select(
+	IN const uintptr_t* Candidates,
+	IN size_t CandidateCount,
+	IN bool PreferLowest,
+	OUT FunctionsArraySelection& Selection,
+	OUT SelectionLog& Log
+)
+{
+	memset(&Log, 0, sizeof(Log));
+
+	// Whatever was in there before must not survive a failure
+	memset(&Selection, 0xCC, sizeof(Selection));
+
+	return GmpiSelectFunctionsArray(
+		Candidates,
+		CandidateCount,
+		GetModuleHandleW(nullptr),
+		FakeCodeFunctionFind,
+		PreferLowest,
+		[&Log, Candidates](size_t Index, uintptr_t Candidate, bool Accepted, size_t EntrySize, const std::string& Text)
+		{
+			if (Log.ReportCount < 16)
+				Log.Reported[Log.ReportCount] = Candidate;
+
+			Log.ReportCount++;
+
+			// A finder looks up the instruction by Index, so it has to be the index of this very candidate
+			if (Candidates[Index] != Candidate)
+				Log.RejectedCount += 1000;
+
+			if (Accepted && EntrySize)
+				Log.AcceptedCount++;
+
+			if (!Accepted && !EntrySize)
+				Log.RejectedCount++;
+
+			if (Text.empty())
+				Log.EmptyTextCount++;
+		},
+		Selection
+	);
+}
+
+// The candidate choice of both finders: GmpFindFunctionsArrayX64 takes the first valid one (PreferLowest false),
+// VM::GmpFindFunctionsArray the valid one lowest in memory (PreferLowest true). Never a fallback.
+// What this cannot reach is the part of the finders that needs Zydis: which instructions become candidates.
+static void TestSelection()
+{
+	ReferentialEntryLayout* referential_table = new ReferentialEntryLayout[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	EmbeddedEntryLayout* embedded_table = new EmbeddedEntryLayout[TABLE_ENTRY_COUNT];
+	PolymorphicDecoy* polymorphic_decoy = new PolymorphicDecoy();
+	BuildReferentialTable(referential_table, g_DefaultNames);
+	BuildEmbeddedTable(embedded_table, g_DefaultNames);
+	SetFinder(FINDER_EXACT, g_DefaultNames);
+
+	// Slots are elements of one array, so a higher index is a higher address
+	g_CandidateSlots[0] = reinterpret_cast<void*>(static_cast<uintptr_t>(0x1122334455667788ull));
+	g_CandidateSlots[1] = nullptr;
+	g_CandidateSlots[2] = polymorphic_decoy;
+	g_CandidateSlots[3] = referential_table;
+	g_CandidateSlots[4] = embedded_table;
+	g_CandidateSlots[5] = referential_table;
+
+	const uintptr_t slot0 = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_CandidateSlots[0]);
+	const uintptr_t slot1 = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_CandidateSlots[1]);
+	const uintptr_t slot2 = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_CandidateSlots[2]);
+	const uintptr_t slot3 = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_CandidateSlots[3]);
+	const uintptr_t slot4 = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_CandidateSlots[4]);
+	const uintptr_t slot5 = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(&g_CandidateSlots[5]);
+
+	FunctionsArraySelection selection;
+	SelectionLog log;
+
+	// Wrong ones first (one of them twice), then a valid one high in memory, then a valid one lower in memory
+	const uintptr_t mixed[] = { slot0, slot1, slot0, slot2, slot4, slot3 };
+
+	// First valid wins
+	Check(Select(mixed, 6, false, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot4 && selection.CandidateIndex == 4 && selection.EntrySize == 80,
+		"select first: three wrong candidates are skipped, the first valid one in list order wins");
+	Check(log.ReportCount == 4 && log.RejectedCount == 3 && log.AcceptedCount == 1 && selection.ExaminedCount == 4,
+		"select first: three rejections and one acceptance are reported, each under its own index");
+	Check(ReportCountOf(log, slot0) == 1, "select first: a candidate that is listed twice is reported once");
+	Check(ReportCountOf(log, slot4) == 1, "control: the winner is among the reported ones");
+	Check(ReportCountOf(log, slot3) == 0, "select first: nothing behind the winner is looked at");
+	Check(log.EmptyTextCount == 0, "select first: every report carries a reason or a summary");
+
+	// Lowest valid wins, and a rejected candidate never counts as "lowest"
+	Check(Select(mixed, 6, true, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot3 && selection.CandidateIndex == 5 && selection.EntrySize == 24,
+		"select lowest: the valid candidate lowest in memory wins, although three rejected ones lie below it");
+	Check(log.ReportCount == 5 && log.RejectedCount == 3 && log.AcceptedCount == 2 && selection.ExaminedCount == 5,
+		"select lowest: every distinct candidate is looked at and reported once");
+
+	// Order, not address, decides in the first mode; address, not order, in the other
+	const uintptr_t low_then_high[] = { slot3, slot4 };
+	const uintptr_t high_then_low[] = { slot4, slot3 };
+	Check(Select(low_then_high, 2, false, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot3 && selection.CandidateIndex == 0,
+		"select first: the lower valid candidate wins when it comes first");
+	Check(Select(high_then_low, 2, false, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot4 && selection.CandidateIndex == 0,
+		"select first: the higher valid candidate wins when it comes first");
+	Check(Select(low_then_high, 2, true, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot3 && selection.CandidateIndex == 0 && selection.EntrySize == 24,
+		"select lowest: the lower valid candidate wins when it comes first");
+	Check(Select(high_then_low, 2, true, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot3 && selection.CandidateIndex == 1 && selection.EntrySize == 24,
+		"select lowest: the lower valid candidate wins when it comes last");
+
+	// A valid candidate that is listed twice is still validated and reported once
+	const uintptr_t valid_twice[] = { slot5, slot0, slot5 };
+	Check(Select(valid_twice, 3, true, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot5 && selection.CandidateIndex == 0,
+		"select lowest: a rejected candidate below the only valid one does not win");
+	Check(log.AcceptedCount == 1 && log.RejectedCount == 1 && log.ReportCount == 2, "select lowest: a valid candidate that is listed twice is reported once");
+
+	// Without a valid one there is no winner, not the least bad one
+	const uintptr_t all_wrong[] = { slot2, slot0, slot1, slot2 };
+	Check(!Select(all_wrong, 4, false, selection, log) && !selection.Candidate && !selection.CandidateIndex && !selection.EntrySize,
+		"select first: nothing is selected if nothing is valid, and what the selection held before is gone");
+	Check(log.ReportCount == 3 && log.RejectedCount == 3 && log.AcceptedCount == 0 && selection.ExaminedCount == 3,
+		"select first: ...but every distinct candidate was reported with its rejection");
+	Check(!Select(all_wrong, 4, true, selection, log) && !selection.Candidate && !selection.EntrySize && selection.ExaminedCount == 3,
+		"select lowest: nothing is selected if nothing is valid");
+
+	// Nothing to choose from
+	Check(!Select(mixed, 0, false, selection, log) && !selection.Candidate && !selection.ExaminedCount && !log.ReportCount,
+		"select first: an empty list selects nothing and reports nothing");
+	Check(!Select(nullptr, 6, true, selection, log) && !selection.Candidate && !selection.ExaminedCount && !log.ReportCount,
+		"select lowest: no list selects nothing, without a fault");
+
+	// Control: the same call selects again as soon as there is something valid
+	Check(Select(low_then_high, 1, false, selection, log) && selection.Candidate == slot3 && log.ReportCount == 1,
+		"control: a list of one valid candidate is selected");
+
+	delete polymorphic_decoy;
+	delete[] embedded_table;
+	delete[] referential_table;
+}
+
+static void TestSearchRepetition()
+{
+	Check(GmpiIsSearchWorthRepeating(0), "repeat: the first search is always run");
+	Check(GmpiIsSearchWorthRepeating(MAXIMUM_FAILED_SEARCHES - 1), "repeat: the last allowed search is run");
+	Check(!GmpiIsSearchWorthRepeating(MAXIMUM_FAILED_SEARCHES), "repeat: after the maximum of failed searches it is not repeated");
+	Check(!GmpiIsSearchWorthRepeating(SIZE_MAX), "repeat: ...and never again after that");
+	Check(MAXIMUM_FAILED_SEARCHES >= 1 && MAXIMUM_FAILED_SEARCHES <= 8, "repeat: the maximum allows at least one search and keeps the log short");
+}
+
+int main()
+{
+	TestNamePredicate();
+	TestReasonCap();
+	TestGuardedReads();
+	TestImagePredicates();
+	TestAcceptedTables();
+	TestRejectedCandidates();
+	TestSelection();
+	TestSearchRepetition();
+
+	printf("%d checks, %d failed\n", g_CheckCount, g_FailureCount);
+	return g_FailureCount ? 1 : 0;
+}
